- `i++` adds one, `i--` takes one, `i += 5` adds five.
- `break;` leaves the loop; `continue;` skips to the next round.
- C has no "for each": arrays are looped with an index, `for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)`.

On an Arduino, `loop()` runs forever because the board's hidden `main` calls it in a `for (;;)` loop. That's why you never write your own endless loop there.

example.code:
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    for (int i = 3; i >= 1; i--) printf("%d\n", i);
    printf("Go!\n");
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) total += i;
    printf("1..10 adds up to %d\n", total);
    return 0;
}

exercises.solution:
int sum_to(int n)
{
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) total += i;
    return total;
}

